{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1500692-the-curse-and-the-glass-walkers","id":1500692,"title":"The Curse and the Glass Walkers.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-01-18T19:44:29-08:00","id":1500692,"title":"The Curse and the Glass Walkers.","content":"Hi! For some time I've wondered about this. Glass Walkers Revised TB says:<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Think about it for a second. What role do most tribes offer their Kin? Financial support for the caern? We\u2019re a tribe that spent the last couple of decades run by those with corporate interests, and even if our budget isn\u2019t unlimited, we\u2019ve no gripping need of donations from our Kinfolk. Or how about sending Kinfolk to the cities to keep an eye out there? No good, we\u2019re in the cities, and sending Kin into the wilderness is exactly the garbage duty most so rightfully hate. Acting as liaisons with humanity? Ever since we were the Warders of Men, that\u2019s been what we do. We\u2019re the tribe that deals with humans better than anyone else, and we can\u2019t afford to let our people skills slip.&quot;</i><br /> \u200b<br /> The character templates in the same book give me a hint to how this is possible despite the Curse. Ragabashs and Theurges are the ones working in companies to make money and in office buildings full of Kinfolk to make a buffer between Garou and humans (Philodoxes gotta work with tougher people, like cops as in the TB's example), or perhaps all employees are Kinfolk (at least those who interact with other businesspersons representing the Garou CEO to other companies or they would be just co-workers to the Garou). Otherwise the Curse would make the company unstable due to employee problems (Galliards and Ahroun are a no-no). At Rank 4 Glass Walkers have the solution to this due the Bury the Wolf Gift and there's also a fetish for that, but the thing it's a level 4 one, not easy or common at all to have laying around.<br /> <br /> So I ask for tips to make the typical Glass Walker business person, approaching each auspice, considering the Curse as described in W20 and the Kinfolk quote from the Glass Walker Revised Tribebook. Kinfolk Unsung Heroes defines the Kinfolk role under a more positive light, they help the Glass Walkers a lot with their disguise, but Kinfolk20 agrees with the TB Revised. If you think Galliards and Ahrouns couldn't do that just express yourself as such.<br /> <br /> I thank you all who contribute here, see ya!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1876-fenixpp","name":"Fenixpp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1876_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-18T20:33:15-08:00","id":1500694,"title":"","content":"Something that might be useful to consider in the modern age is whether you/your ST rules the Curse as being active across technological barriers (phone calls, recorded messages, zoom, etc.). Furthermore this post isn\u2019t made with the mechanics of particular gifts in mind, but rather just off the top of my head thematically.<br /> <br /> So what we\u2019re assuming here is that we\u2019re dealing with a standard sort of Glass Walker who views being able to interact with the humans as part of their tribal purview (although I have to imagine that some would be more concerned with the larger infrastructure of humanity rather then individuals, which is an area that the Children of Gaia are also notably involved in). That says though, let\u2019s consider how some of these Children of Cockroach might interact with humans in a daily setting.<br /> <br /> Ragabash are the auspice that have the most wiggle room imo when it comes to how they go about fulfilling their spiritual obligations (although it may also be noted that the predominantly Homid tribe, given that homid\u2019s start with Gnosis 1, might not be as concerned with spiritual obligations and taboos as some other tribes). Ragabash are able to interact with the largest section of humanity due to their starting Rage and they come equipped with multiple gifts which help them influence social settings. I imagine that the Glass Walker Ragabash are the ones who are assigned the most socially dependent roles in human-affairs. The Ragabash are the ones sweet-talking clients, shmoozing at parties, doing prep work on deals, etc. Their is also a significant number of them involved with corporate espionage and similar affairs.<br /> <br /> Theurges have two things going for them imo. They have the second best ability to interact with humans* on a rage level. The reason I qualify that statement is because of their other quality. They are also the ones who work with spirits the most. It\u2019s their obligation more then any other Glass Walkers to handle the other side of life, the spiritual. This spreads to every aspect of life. They protect the tribe\u2019s assets with wards and guardians, they can bring prosperity by forming alliances with the right spirits, they can damage rivals in the same way, they can use spirits to not only provide information but to theoretically do any other thing that you might imagine. This is all phenomenally useful. The issue is that it doesn\u2019t come for free. The theurges must either run roughshod over the spirits or negotiate and offer chiminage. Individuals who have chiminage with multiple spirits may become quite eccentric and this may easily interfere in their ability to operate socially with outsiders. Thus I imagine many GW Theurges are people who offer support from relative isolation or in positions that are allowed their eccentricities; they may be executives, skilled labor (Ex: programmers), creatives, or any other role which will not meddle with their spiritual obligations.<br /> <br /> Philodox are on the edge between those who can be reasonably considered to be social with humanity and those who cannot. The Philodox also have a particular set of skills and instruction to help reinforce their ability to use them. They can keep the camp running smoothly. I imagine the Philodox filling the roles of managers, Human Resources, counter-espionage, and negotiation. Their traditional role as \u2018peace leaders\u2019 and their training for leadership and mediation helps them fulfill managerial tasks (although the Curse is a mixed blessing in this respect; few Philodox managers are loved by their workers, but they are taken seriously). The Philodox can also be expected to keep tabs of those within their circle and monitor them for any signs of friction or unusual activity, making them prime fodder for HR or Counter-Espionage. With regards to negotiation, I imagine this will be a case by case basis. In some cases the light-touch of a Ragabash will be better suited. In some cases the no-nonsense Philodox will be the better candidate.<br /> <br /> The Galliard is where things begin to get somewhat dicey with regards to humanity. Their Curse can be trusted to disturb most people, but that doesn\u2019t mean the Galliard doesn\u2019t have roles they can fulfill. For one, depending on your interpretation of the Curse, their tendency to be charismatic and skilled performers can make them valuable public faces. Imagine a Steve Jobs-esqe figure, a charismatic figurehead who is feared and loathed by those who have to work directly with them. I can also imagine the Galliard fulfilling Garou-facing work, carrying out the order of the tribal leaders and making sure that the Glass Walkers are all on the same page. For most Galliard GW, the path will likely be that of one of keeping their fellow Garou on good terms or in some creative field where they can be kept isolated from the masses of humanity.<br /> <br /> the Ahroun are the least likely to be accepted by the rank and file humans and thus have to be handled delicately. Some GW operations might not have them interact with humans at all, which effectively creates the kinfolk job of \u2018Ahroun Handler\u2019 (which they\u2019re only somewhat less uneasy about then a human would be). In the occasions where Ahroun do get a position on the mortal stage, it will probably be in very particular scenarios. Want to give the implication that some of your group\u2019s silent partners are on another side of the law? Ahroun mobster. Want to have your company operate an internal security force? It\u2019s managed by the Ahroun, who are the hardest motherfuckers there. Are you in an area where you might be interacting with local warlords or generals for some reason? Ahrouns may be the ones you want representing your interests. More than any other auspice though, the Ahroun is an auspice to be handled with care around humanity.<br /> <br /> Hope this helped and that you get the info you\u2019re looking for!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-19T12:47:38-08:00","id":1500760,"title":"","content":"Such valuable insights! Thanks for such thorough answer and for your time!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1876-fenixpp","name":"Fenixpp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1876_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-19T14:56:26-08:00","id":1500775,"title":"","content":"I think it's important to keep in mind that sometimes a writer is, to be blunt, wrong. &quot;We don't need Kinfolk we're loaded and can handle humans ourselves&quot; is something a Glass Walker might say, sure, but they'd be wrong. Undervaluing Kinfolk is par for the course in and out of universe.<br /> <br /> Writers will simply forget that Glass Walkers and Bone Gnawers have Ahroun and Galliards. It's that simple.<br /> <br /> The other points made are good ones though!\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-19T15:46:18-08:00","id":1500780,"title":"","content":"I was just trying to piece together how this view of the game would develop considering the Curse, but your point it's solid indeed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1876-fenixpp","name":"Fenixpp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1876_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-19T15:52:37-08:00","id":1500781,"title":"","content":"I think it's not even the writer being wrong, it's the speaker being a pompous overconfident git. It's clear that it's an in-character section and as such it should be taken with a pinch of salt.<br /> <br /> i feel that the Glass Walkers may be the tribe that's most in tune with their kinfolk and that have organised their business and dealings on all level to have kinfolk as their trusted people and right hands. Their reliance on tech requires experts and knowhow that not all packmembers can have and I can see certain packs having kinfolk support teams - be it armorers, mission control, work crews, lawyer offices and assorted other things that allow them to work on the urban battlefield.<br /> <br /> The Dies Ultimae are especially interesting here as they have actually promoted their kinfolk into full on soldiers and operate with packs that consist of combinations of werewolves and highly trained soldier kinfolk in the field.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-19T20:06:34-08:00","id":1500805,"title":"","content":"Writers of the source material often seem to forget about the Curse and its impact on the setting. Regardless, we still need an idea of how the Glass Walkers can build influence in mortal society.<br /> <br /> In my chronicles, the Glass Walker Garou tend to stay within private businesses and surround themselves with kinfolk. Privately owned businesses can still become big and rich, but tend to get less scrutiny. Since they also start small, it's easier to grow the business from a basis of kinfolk only (primarily their own tribal kin, but also including Bone Gnawer clan and to a lesser degree open to other tribes as well). When they do need to hire outsiders, they can make sure they stay at unimportant levels and don't interact with the &quot;bosses&quot; or they can stick them in some annex or remote office doing support work that never brings them in contact with the main office where the Garou might be.<br /> <br /> Their businesses also tend to be local - based in one city or with regional offices. They aren't national so they don't have to hire many non-kinfolk who may get promoted or begin to ask questions on why they aren't being given opportunities. They also cooperate substantially with other Glass Walker or kinfolk owned enterprises, limiting the contact with outsiders. If they need financing, they go to some bank that already has Glass Walkers in it. If they need financial audits done they use kinfolk accountants. Such businesses also make it less likely for vampires, PENTEX, or other enemies to attempt to exploit or take over those enterprises because they seem less viable.<br /> <br /> In some type of organizations, it isn't necessary for the Garou owners to mix with ordinary people. If you own a restaurant, you can have one kinfolk manage it, and it won't matter how many humans he has working for him. If you own a chain, most locations may not even need any kinfolk and run completely independently. A Garou who owns a local pizza chain may only need to staff kinfolk at the main office and maybe one actual location (if he really wants to pick up some pizza himself). If you own a local bank with various branch locations, most of those branches could be staffed completely with humans who never come in contact with &quot;the board&quot; or &quot;the investors&quot;. You can do the same with real estate and various other businesses. That's also a good way to network with kinfolk of other tribes - some isolated kinfolk only village of the Fianna might appreciate the opportunity to work in a local bank branch (owned by the Glass Walkers) that can provide them a source of loans and investment while the Glass Walkers can expand and make good contacts.<br /> <br /> And of course, high rage Garou might choose to enter careers that are filled with dangerous people. That's probably one reason the Glass Walkers are known to be involved in organized crime. In a normal business, you don't want people terrified of you or think you are a psycho killer. If you work in the criminal underworld, that reputation may be beneficial. There are also occupations where it isn't necessary to have much social interaction with people.<br /> <br /> So if possible keep it kinfolk only. If not, surround the Garou with kinfolk, and keep the humans off site as much as possible. If the business gets bigger, make sure kinfolk are always at key positions to act as gatekeepers. It's all about limiting the exposure of humans to Garou, especially high rage Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-20T07:19:06-08:00","id":1500837,"title":"","content":"As people have covered it's probably a mixture of small businesses being staffed primarily by kinfolk, larger businesses having the Garou far enough upstairs that they don't have to interact with the normal humans, and the low-Rage Garou being the ones actually doing a lot of the business.<br /> <br /> A lot of the issues come from the fact that the time of 1e was messy, and they were still working things out (Klaives didn't exist in the earliest days!). The writers wanted werewolves to have difficulty interacting with humans, they wanted a Tribe of high tech Garou who run businesses, and nobody sat down and thought about how they'd interact. The simple answer would be to offload a lot of the work to Kinfolk, but the GW have terrible Garou-Kinfolk relations. So we have to assume that a lot of them keep their Rage low or move into skilled professions where it is much less of an issue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:blue\">Blue is sarcasm.</span><br /> <br /> If I suggestion I make contradicts in-setting metaphysics please ignore me, I probably brought in scientific ideas.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4507-twodsix","name":"TwoDSix","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"833"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-30T11:26:46-07:00","id":1506964,"title":"","content":"Playing a glasswalker Ahroun, in a pack with a glasswalker Theurge, I've really been struggling with this. Both characters are fairly high WP so don't have to deal with the social dice penalty from rage very often, and being homid glasswalkers they're fairly low Gnosis and so they don't have to deal with getting enraged by seeing people litter or from the smell of synthetic detergents, but I think the ST and I have both been struggling with how to portray the curse. <br /> <br /> About 50% of people want to avoid you at rage 5, but is the effect more pronounced for people of lower willpower? Is it still perceptible (maybe with an awareness roll or the like?) for humans with high enough willpower to not be affected? Like are they totally unaffected or is it that they're willful enough to shrug it off? If the latter is it linear or logarithmic. If a WP 3 human dealing with a rage 3 philodox can still feel the curse but choose not to acknowledge it, then can a WP 4 human feel it but less, or is it so slight as to be imperceptible to her?<br /> <br /> It's implied that repeated experience to delirium can build up a resistance in some people, leading to false positives on thinking folks are kinfolk or the like, but could repeated contact with the curse be similar? Could a person just habituate to it if you're consistently not dangerous despite their instincts telling them otherwise?<br /> <br /> Also the ST has been running it mostly as microagressions, people taking a half step away or crossing the street when walking opposite the Ahroun, people locking doors as she goes by doors or cars, clutching loved ones close or gripping things like improvised weapons. Quietly avoiding the character or attempting to overlook her presence looking to pass her engagement on to co-workers or supervisors. And I dig it, this garou in particular was very social as a Cub and is taking the isolation really hard.<br /> <br /> But I can't help but wonder should it be more pronounced? More than once I've had the character sneak up on someone and taken advantage of the curse to really *surprise* them (more than a little sadistically). But I wonder if theres a guideline, I think the ST is being appropriate but I feel like the book description also implies a more Fight/Flight/Freeze response, like she walks into a room and some people just bail, leaving meetings, perhaps forgetting to pack up their things, just *gone*. And to give away part of the setting, the sept elders have decided to have the cliaths attend classes at a local school, so I'm wondering about the fight response from people who realize that they'll have to be subjected to being in a room with this dangerous person over and over again. Like do they pick a fight with her to try and make her leave? Do they try and pin stuff on her to try and get the authorities/staff to make her leave? Fuck with her stuff/desk while she isn't around in hopes of making her feel unwelcome? Or is all of that sort of thing too elaborate for the Curse?<br /> <br /> I've mostly read w20 books so I don't know if there's more guidance in some older players guide or ST handbook but I feel kind of lost.<br /> <br /> So far I've gotten by with using kinfolk/high-WP catspaws and liberal use of the Persuasion gift (which the ST has started to punish me for by leaning on the &quot;significantly more impact&quot; line of the system text) Also a couple times by putting myself on the same 'team' as humans and using the Inspiration gift to grant them temp-WP high enough to be comfortable. I can imagine similar tactics working for more experienced GlassWalkers. Also glancing through Wyld West supplements it seems like a medicine bag fetish could be used to bypass the curse, so I wonder if some weaver spirit fetish or talen could be convinced to do similar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-30T11:54:53-07:00","id":1506968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Devidramoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1500692-the-curse-and-the-glass-walkers?p=1506964#post1506964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Playing a glasswalker Ahroun, in a pack with a glasswalker Theurge, I've really been struggling with this. Both characters are fairly high WP so don't have to deal with the social dice penalty from rage very often, and being homid glasswalkers they're fairly low Gnosis and so they don't have to deal with getting enraged by seeing people litter or from the smell of synthetic detergents...</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not how that works. Anything that involves frenzy and frenzy triggers involves Rage. Most GWs don't frenzy around litter because they're acclimated to it and there are spirits that go along with it in an urban milieu.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> About 50% of people want to avoid you at rage 5, but is the effect more pronounced for people of lower willpower? Is it still perceptible (maybe with an awareness roll or the like?) for humans with high enough willpower to not be affected? Like are they totally unaffected or is it that they're willful enough to shrug it off? If the latter is it linear or logarithmic. If a WP 3 human dealing with a rage 3 philodox can still feel the curse but choose not to acknowledge it, then can a WP 4 human feel it but less, or is it so slight as to be imperceptible to her? </div> </div> </div>The Curse is rather binary. Either people don't want to be around you, or they have no problems.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's implied that repeated experience to delirium can build up a resistance in some people, leading to false positives on thinking folks are kinfolk or the like, but could repeated contact with the curse be similar? Could a person just habituate to it if you're consistently not dangerous despite their instincts telling them otherwise? </div> </div> </div>Not unless they take up a serious interest in the occult and hedge sorcery (a path at four dots or more makes you partially awakened), become a supernatural being, or the like. If you want Wraith crossover, several repeated near-death experiences might also do the trick, which would also turn the human in question into a projector (basically, they're still alive, but their living spirit and consciousness can leap out of their body in a form very much like a ghost). Generally, most people would find this unpleasant and extremely time-consuming (also money-consuming, as both occult texts and repeated resuscitation by health professionals can get extremely pricey).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also the ST has been running it mostly as microagressions, people taking a half step away or crossing the street when walking opposite the Ahroun, people locking doors as she goes by doors or cars, clutching loved ones close or gripping things like improvised weapons. Quietly avoiding the character or attempting to overlook her presence looking to pass her engagement on to co-workers or supervisors. And I dig it, this garou in particular was very social as a Cub and is taking the isolation really hard.<br /> <br /> But I can't help but wonder should it be more pronounced? More than once I've had the character sneak up on someone and taken advantage of the curse to really *surprise* them (more than a little sadistically). But I wonder if theres a guideline, I think the ST is being appropriate but I feel like the book description also implies a more Fight/Flight/Freeze response, like she walks into a room and some people just bail, leaving meetings, perhaps forgetting to pack up their things, just *gone*. And to give away part of the setting, the sept elders have decided to have the cliaths attend classes at a local school, so I'm wondering about the fight response from people who realize that they'll have to be subjected to being in a room with this dangerous person over and over again. Like do they pick a fight with her to try and make her leave? Do they try and pin stuff on her to try and get the authorities/staff to make her leave? Fuck with her stuff/desk while she isn't around in hopes of making her feel unwelcome? Or is all of that sort of thing too elaborate for the Curse? </div> </div> </div>Probably more along the line of prolonged stress effects - they lose sleep or outright get insomnia, get sick more often, and generally seem like they have PTSD even if nothing in their life would suggest that they should. The Curse and The Delirium are a species-wide PTSD effect born into humanity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've mostly read w20 books so I don't know if there's more guidance in some older players guide or ST handbook but I feel kind of lost.<br /> <br /> So far I've gotten by with using kinfolk/high-WP catspaws and liberal use of the Persuasion gift (which the ST has started to punish me for by leaning on the &quot;significantly more impact&quot; line of the system text) Also a couple times by putting myself on the same 'team' as humans and using the Inspiration gift to grant them temp-WP high enough to be comfortable. I can imagine similar tactics working for more experienced GlassWalkers. Also glancing through Wyld West supplements it seems like a medicine bag fetish could be used to bypass the curse, so I wonder if some weaver spirit fetish or talen could be convinced to do similar. </div> </div> </div>Not without Weaver-related unpleasantness. Keep in mind that in modern times, the Weaver's answer to &quot;How do I stop inflicting the Curse?&quot; is &quot;Listen to the OneSong, become a drone.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-30T12:40:12-07:00","id":1506975,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's not how that works. Anything that involves frenzy and frenzy triggers involves Rage. Most GWs don't frenzy around litter because they're acclimated to it and there are spirits that go along with it in an urban milieu. </div> </div> </div>Didnt mean to imply that those things would normally lead to frenzy rolls, was more from the reading that gnosis makes you more aware of the connections of things, and being a garou after your first change changes your interpretations of your senses, that, say a metis silver fang would be more struck by the unnaturalness of artificial scents, deodorants and detergents and would be less able to look away from the global impact of casual negligence of littering that would lead to increased social tension on the Garou's side when dealing with humans which could, in turn, exacerbate problems created by the Rage penalty and the Curse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-30T13:27:09-07:00","id":1506985,"title":"","content":"I use several guidelines with the Curse to incorporate it as an important part of the game, but not utterly paralyze things.<br /> <br /> First, I use the traditional Willpower percentages table of Werewolf to reflect the entire population. A lot of people with very low Willpower will be small children and the elderly. They don't reflect the typical interaction with adults you find on the city streets. So if a Garou walks downs the city street, then a small child will cling to their mother for protection, but the mother will think it is more an issue with the child than the stranger the child seems to fear. If a Garou enters a bar, there may not be anyone impacted by the Curse even if according to the Willpower table there should be people who are impacted. In contrast, if the Garou enters a grammar school or retirement home, most of the people there would be affected even by a low Rage Garou.<br /> <br /> Second, I do interpret reactions of people affected by the Curse liberally. They do sense danger, but how they react to it depends. Some of them might think they are just being irrational and try their best to ignore it. Others might remove themselves from the situation. Others will just begin to keep their eyes on the person to see what they do. A cop might just encourage the Garou to keep moving. A storeowner may go up to the Garou and see how they can help them... so they can leave the story as soon as possible. Somebody else might start complaining the Garou is &quot;looking at them&quot; and try to get someone who will help them get the Garou to leave. My goal is to acknowledge the Curse in game as a flavor text, but not cause the scene to end or frustrate what the player is trying to do.<br /> <br /> Third, I keep in mind that many NPCs will spend their 1 point of WP to ignore the impact of the Curse for that scene. They don't want to show they are scared, panic, break ranks, etc. just because they have an irrational feeling.<br /> <br /> Fourth, I also use a House Rule that the Garou can spend 1 WP to &quot;bury the wolf&quot; for a scene. So if they want to meet someone at a public restaurant, we can have that scene and not have the Curse ruin what could be fun for the game.<br /> <br /> Fifth, I remind players that this is a good reason to have the Kinfolk background. Maybe it isn't the actual PC who goes out and does that stuff. It is one of their Kinfolk they go with the Kinfolk background. Have them develop that Kinfolk as a real person. That way the player can always be active, even if their Garou PC sits out that scene.<br /> <br /> I tend to try to keep Garou with Rage 5 or lower (all starting characters) to be able to accomplish most things they may want to do. The Curse is more flavor text than anything, but with the understanding that sometimes the PC may need to spend some effort to mitigate the circumstances. However, if the PC wants to increase Rage to 6 or higher, then I work with them in advance so they can make the necessary preparations for the changes in their life that it will require. A Garou with Rage 8 should definitely be leading to very different scenes when they interact with the Flock.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}